  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved resilience to transboundary sand and dust storms in Kuwait and Iraq</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sand and dust storms are causing significant socioeconomic damages to Kuwait, with estimated economic disruptions of up to USD 190 million annually and severe health impacts on its population. To reduce sand and dust storm events in Kuwait, it is critical to prevent and mitigate desertification in areas where sand and dust storms are originating, i.e., Southern Iraq. Simultaneously, measures should also be taken in Kuwait to minimize the impact of sand and dust storms and mitigate multiple challenges caused by them. 

The project aims to improve resilience of cities and communities in Kuwait and southern Iraq to transboundary sand and dust storms through:

EA/Outcome 1: Improved knowledge and understanding on the conditions that originate sand and dust storms, and their impact on Human Health as well as prevention and mitigation measures.

EA/Outcome 2: Restored ecosystem in the origin areas of sand and dust storms in southern Iraq.

EA/Outcome 3: Enhanced resilience and capacity of authorities and communities in southern Iraq to adapt to the impact of desertification and mitigate the causes of sandstorms resulted from climate change.</narrative>
  </description>
